        <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">Our experience with coding agents so far points to a future where machines can move quickly, but good engineering still depends on people who know what “right” means.</p>
        <ul class="principles">
          <li><strong>Agents are useful teammates.</strong> Let them tackle boilerplate, tests, scripts, configurations, debugging clues, and first drafts.</li>
          <li><strong>Context is the human advantage.</strong> Agents can miss why a system exists, what users need, and which trade-offs matter.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear specifications save time.</strong> Breaking a problem into explicit tasks helps both people and agents do better work.</li>
          <li><strong>Review and verification are core engineering.</strong> Check correctness, usability, security, and operations before trusting the output.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="disagreement">
          <h3>One friendly disagreement: how much should agents run on their own?</h3>
          <p><strong>More autonomy:</strong> Keval can imagine agents deploying, monitoring, and fixing systems when engineers give them clear permissions, tests, and rollback plans.</p>
          <p><strong>More caution:</strong> Nirav emphasizes defining correctness and reviewing every line, while Harsh stresses that agents still lack the context behind a system’s design.</p>
          <p>Either way, handing an agent a vague task and production access is not a strategy. It is a very fast way to learn what your rollback plan is for.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="closing-note">Our shared prediction: software engineering shifts from writing every line to specifying, directing, reviewing, and verifying the work—and the clearest thinker in the room remains a valuable teammate.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
